      "id": "wfm371t",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Foundation of the Delhi Sultanate"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Delhi Sultanate was established in 1206 AD following the Ghurid conquests in Northern India.",
        "Five successive dynasties ruled: Slave (Mamluk), Khilji, Tughlaq, Sayyid, and Lodi.",
        "Alauddin Khilji implemented strict market control policies and introduced the Daag (branding horses) and Chehra (descriptive roll) systems.",
        "Muhammad bin Tughlaq introduced token currency and shifted the capital from Delhi to Devagiri (Daulatabad).",
        "The Sultanate period ended in 1526 when Babur defeated Ibrahim Lodi in the First Battle of Panipat."
      ],
      "id": "zxrj0kh"
    },
